  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C008">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C008: Binding_pocket - provides reference point for - Apo_holo_classification</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_holo_classification"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/provides_reference_point_for"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Binding_pocket"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>AHoJ-DB treats the binding pocket, not the full protein structure, as the reference point for apo/holo assignment. This makes classification more precise for proteins with multiple binding sites.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C070">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C070: AHoJ_DB - can support - Binding_site_analysis_use_case</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Binding_site_analysis_use_case"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/can_support"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Because AHoJ-DB compares apo and holo pocket geometries, it can support analysis of cryptic binding sites. Higher apo pocket RMSD and smaller apo pocket volume may indicate hidden or ligand-induced binding-site behavior.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C101">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C101: Apo_PLP_binding_sites_16 - can be used to study - PLP_binding_variability</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/PLP_binding_variability"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/can_be_used_to_study"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_PLP_binding_sites_16"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>The 16 apo PLP-binding sites can be used to study variability of PLP binding.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The search by UniProt AC provides an overview of known binding sites of the protein and a number of biologically relevant ligands found. It can be compared to the information provided by the PDBe-KB; if we look at glycogen phosphorylase from Oryctolagus cuniculus (UniProt AC: P00489 – see Figure 1) we can see 499 unique protein–ligand interactions – these represent 193 unique ligands, while PDBe-KB provides information about 198 known ligands – five of those are not biologically relevant according to BioLiP2 database. The result also shows us that the most common ligand is PLP that is forming 284 unique interactions in 130 structures. There are 16 sites corresponding to PLP-binding sites in APO form – it can be therefore used to study the variability of PLP binding and detect conformational changes between apo and holo structures. The results also show solvent accessible surface area calculated for the binding site (SASA), and the SASA normalized by the number of amino acids in the binding site that allows the analysis of the binding sites. More detailed information and visualizations of apo holo pairs are available through the 'View' or 'Download' buttons.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Non_biologically_relevant_ligands_5">
    <rdfs:label>5 non-biologically relevant ligands</rdfs:label>
    <ahoj:reportedValue rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</ahoj:reportedValue>
    <ahoj:unit>ligands</ahoj:unit>
    <pred:are_not_biologically_relevant_according_to rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/BioLiP2"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C108">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C108: Apo_holo_pair_visualizations - are available through - Download_button</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Download_button"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/are_available_through"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_holo_pair_visualizations"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Detailed apo-holo pair information is available through the Download button.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The search by UniProt AC provides an overview of known binding sites of the protein and a number of biologically relevant ligands found. It can be compared to the information provided by the PDBe-KB; if we look at glycogen phosphorylase from Oryctolagus cuniculus (UniProt AC: P00489 – see Figure 1) we can see 499 unique protein–ligand interactions – these represent 193 unique ligands, while PDBe-KB provides information about 198 known ligands – five of those are not biologically relevant according to BioLiP2 database. The result also shows us that the most common ligand is PLP that is forming 284 unique interactions in 130 structures. There are 16 sites corresponding to PLP-binding sites in APO form – it can be therefore used to study the variability of PLP binding and detect conformational changes between apo and holo structures. The results also show solvent accessible surface area calculated for the binding site (SASA), and the SASA normalized by the number of amino acids in the binding site that allows the analysis of the binding sites. More detailed information and visualizations of apo holo pairs are available through the 'View' or 'Download' buttons.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C092">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C092: Glycogen_phosphorylase_Oryctolagus_cuniculus - has unique protein ligand interactions - Unique_protein_ligand_interactions_499</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Unique_protein_ligand_interactions_499"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/has_unique_protein_ligand_interactions"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Glycogen_phosphorylase_Oryctolagus_cuniculus"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>For glycogen phosphorylase from Oryctolagus cuniculus, the AHoJ-DB result shows 499 unique protein-ligand interactions.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The search by UniProt AC provides an overview of known binding sites of the protein and a number of biologically relevant ligands found. It can be compared to the information provided by the PDBe-KB; if we look at glycogen phosphorylase from Oryctolagus cuniculus (UniProt AC: P00489 – see Figure 1) we can see 499 unique protein–ligand interactions – these represent 193 unique ligands, while PDBe-KB provides information about 198 known ligands – five of those are not biologically relevant according to BioLiP2 database. The result also shows us that the most common ligand is PLP that is forming 284 unique interactions in 130 structures. There are 16 sites corresponding to PLP-binding sites in APO form – it can be therefore used to study the variability of PLP binding and detect conformational changes between apo and holo structures. The results also show solvent accessible surface area calculated for the binding site (SASA), and the SASA normalized by the number of amino acids in the binding site that allows the analysis of the binding sites. More detailed information and visualizations of apo holo pairs are available through the 'View' or 'Download' buttons.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/External_KB_mapping_correction_2026_06_11">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/MappingSet"/>
    <rdfs:label>correction of external KB mappings: no empty-prefix targets and corrected ligand ChEBI mapping</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:source rdf:resource="file:///mnt/data/ahoj_paper_claims_knowledge_graph_reduced_entities_external_kb_enriched.ttl"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C013">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C013: AHoJ_DB_input_selection - requires - UniProt_accession</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/UniProt_accession"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/requires"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB_input_selection"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Interactions without UniProt accession assignment were excluded because the method searches for candidate structures within the same UniProt accession. UniProt is therefore essential for grouping comparable structures.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/BioLiP2">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ExternalResource"/>
    <rdfs:label>BioLiP2</rdfs:label>
    <skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="https://zhanggroup.org/BioLiP/"/>
    <skos:scopeNote>BioLiP/BioLiP2 protein-ligand interaction resource.</skos:scopeNote>
    <ahoj:mappedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/External_KB_mapping_enrichment_2026_06_11"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C037">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C037: AHoJ_DB - processed - Protein_ligand_interaction</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Protein_ligand_interaction"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/processed"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>The paper reports that AHoJ-DB processed 468,293 biologically relevant protein-ligand interactions. Each processed interaction corresponds to a separate entry and query binding site.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/mappingSubject">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Property"/>
    <rdfs:label>mapping subject</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C082">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C082: Ligand_search_use_case - can reveal - Ligand_apo_coverage</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Ligand_apo_coverage"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/can_reveal"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Ligand_search_use_case"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Searching by ligand name or ID can show how many binding sites for that ligand have apo counterparts. The paper uses bacteriochlorophyll A as an example of low apo coverage.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C081">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C081: UniProt_search_use_case - can reveal - UniProt_search_use_case</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/UniProt_search_use_case"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/can_reveal"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/UniProt_search_use_case"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Searching by UniProt accession provides an overview of known binding sites and biologically relevant ligands for a protein. The paper illustrates this with glycogen phosphorylase.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/often_lack">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Property"/>
    <rdfs:label>often lack</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C001">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C001: Protein_structure - rarely captures - Protein_conformational_variability</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Protein_conformational_variability"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/rarely_captures"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Protein_structure"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>A single experimentally determined protein structure is usually insufficient to describe the conformational variability of a protein. Both apo and holo structures are needed to understand protein geometry and make meaningful comparisons.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C087">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C087: ent:PLP_binding_site - is binding site of - ent:Glycogen_phosphorylase_Oryctolagus_cuniculus</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Glycogen_phosphorylase_Oryctolagus_cuniculus"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/is_binding_site_of"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/PLP_binding_site"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>The PLP binding site is represented as a binding site associated with the glycogen phosphorylase protein example.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The search by UniProt AC provides an overview of known binding sites of the protein and a number of biologically relevant ligands found. It can be compared to the information provided by the PDBe-KB; if we look at glycogen phosphorylase from Oryctolagus cuniculus (UniProt AC: P00489 – see Figure 1) we can see 499 unique protein–ligand interactions – these represent 193 unique ligands, while PDBe-KB provides information about 198 known ligands – five of those are not biologically relevant according to BioLiP2 database. The result also shows us that the most common ligand is PLP that is forming 284 unique interactions in 130 structures. There are 16 sites corresponding to PLP-binding sites in APO form – it can be therefore used to study the variability of PLP binding and detect conformational changes between apo and holo structures. The results also show solvent accessible surface area calculated for the binding site (SASA), and the SASA normalized by the number of amino acids in the binding site that allows the analysis of the binding sites. More detailed information and visualizations of apo holo pairs are available through the 'View' or 'Download' buttons.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>
