  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C099">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C099: PLP_unique_interactions_284 - occur in - PLP_structures_130</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/PLP_structures_130"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/occur_in"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/PLP_unique_interactions_284"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>The 284 unique PLP interactions occur in 130 structures.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The search by UniProt AC provides an overview of known binding sites of the protein and a number of biologically relevant ligands found. It can be compared to the information provided by the PDBe-KB; if we look at glycogen phosphorylase from Oryctolagus cuniculus (UniProt AC: P00489 – see Figure 1) we can see 499 unique protein–ligand interactions – these represent 193 unique ligands, while PDBe-KB provides information about 198 known ligands – five of those are not biologically relevant according to BioLiP2 database. The result also shows us that the most common ligand is PLP that is forming 284 unique interactions in 130 structures. There are 16 sites corresponding to PLP-binding sites in APO form – it can be therefore used to study the variability of PLP binding and detect conformational changes between apo and holo structures. The results also show solvent accessible surface area calculated for the binding site (SASA), and the SASA normalized by the number of amino acids in the binding site that allows the analysis of the binding sites. More detailed information and visualizations of apo holo pairs are available through the 'View' or 'Download' buttons.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>
