    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>In the unfiltered database, 230,269 of 468,293 entries have at least one apo form. This corresponds to 49.2% of processed entries.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_holo_conformational_changes">
    <rdfs:label>conformational changes between apo and holo structures</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C017">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C017: Query_pocket - uses distance cutoff - Distance_cutoff_4_5_angstrom</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Distance_cutoff_4_5_angstrom"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/uses_distance_cutoff"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Query_pocket"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>AHoJ-DB defines binding residues using a 4.5 Å distance cutoff around the query ligand. The same cutoff is later used to detect ligands near mapped candidate pockets.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C061">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C061: AHoJ_DB - is compatible with - Interface_binding_pocket</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Interface_binding_pocket"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/is_compatible_with"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>AHoJ-DB can handle binding sites formed by multiple chains. This allows the database to represent apo/holo relationships for pockets at protein-protein interfaces.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C072">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C072: AHoJ_DB - can support - Binding_site_analysis_use_case</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Binding_site_analysis_use_case"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/can_support"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>The paper states that AHoJ-DB can help investigate order-to-disorder transitions. This is supported by its tracking of observed and unobserved residues in mapped pockets.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C091">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C091: Glycogen_phosphorylase_Oryctolagus_cuniculus - has UniProt accession - UniProt_P00489</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/UniProt_P00489"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/has_UniProt_accession"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Glycogen_phosphorylase_Oryctolagus_cuniculus"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Glycogen phosphorylase from Oryctolagus cuniculus is identified by UniProt AC P00489.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The search by UniProt AC provides an overview of known binding sites of the protein and a number of biologically relevant ligands found. It can be compared to the information provided by the PDBe-KB; if we look at glycogen phosphorylase from Oryctolagus cuniculus (UniProt AC: P00489 – see Figure 1) we can see 499 unique protein–ligand interactions – these represent 193 unique ligands, while PDBe-KB provides information about 198 known ligands – five of those are not biologically relevant according to BioLiP2 database. The result also shows us that the most common ligand is PLP that is forming 284 unique interactions in 130 structures. There are 16 sites corresponding to PLP-binding sites in APO form – it can be therefore used to study the variability of PLP binding and detect conformational changes between apo and holo structures. The results also show solvent accessible surface area calculated for the binding site (SASA), and the SASA normalized by the number of amino acids in the binding site that allows the analysis of the binding sites. More detailed information and visualizations of apo holo pairs are available through the 'View' or 'Download' buttons.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C025">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C025: Solvent_or_non_solvent_ligand - are excluded from - Apo_holo_classification</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_holo_classification"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/are_excluded_from"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Solvent_or_non_solvent_ligand"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>The apo/holo classification ignores solvent molecules. Only non-solvent ligands near the mapped pocket determine holo status.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
