  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C098">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C098: PLP - forms - PLP_unique_interactions_284</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/PLP_unique_interactions_284"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/forms"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/PLP"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>PLP forms 284 unique interactions.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The search by UniProt AC provides an overview of known binding sites of the protein and a number of biologically relevant ligands found. It can be compared to the information provided by the PDBe-KB; if we look at glycogen phosphorylase from Oryctolagus cuniculus (UniProt AC: P00489 – see Figure 1) we can see 499 unique protein–ligand interactions – these represent 193 unique ligands, while PDBe-KB provides information about 198 known ligands – five of those are not biologically relevant according to BioLiP2 database. The result also shows us that the most common ligand is PLP that is forming 284 unique interactions in 130 structures. There are 16 sites corresponding to PLP-binding sites in APO form – it can be therefore used to study the variability of PLP binding and detect conformational changes between apo and holo structures. The results also show solvent accessible surface area calculated for the binding site (SASA), and the SASA normalized by the number of amino acids in the binding site that allows the analysis of the binding sites. More detailed information and visualizations of apo holo pairs are available through the 'View' or 'Download' buttons.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://zhanggroup.org/BioLiP/">
    <rdfs:label>BioLiP/BioLiP2</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C020">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C020: Mapped_candidate_pocket - corresponds to - Query_pocket</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Query_pocket"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/corresponds_to"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Mapped_candidate_pocket"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>A mapped candidate pocket is the equivalent pocket region in another PDB structure. It corresponds to the query pocket by residue mapping, not necessarily by identical 3D geometry.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C086">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C086: ent:Glycogen_phosphorylase_Oryctolagus_cuniculus - has known binding site - ent:PLP_binding_site</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/PLP_binding_site"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/has_known_binding_site"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Glycogen_phosphorylase_Oryctolagus_cuniculus"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>The AHoJ-DB use-case discussion links the glycogen phosphorylase example to known PLP binding-site information.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The search by UniProt AC provides an overview of known binding sites of the protein and a number of biologically relevant ligands found. It can be compared to the information provided by the PDBe-KB; if we look at glycogen phosphorylase from Oryctolagus cuniculus (UniProt AC: P00489 – see Figure 1) we can see 499 unique protein–ligand interactions – these represent 193 unique ligands, while PDBe-KB provides information about 198 known ligands – five of those are not biologically relevant according to BioLiP2 database. The result also shows us that the most common ligand is PLP that is forming 284 unique interactions in 130 structures. There are 16 sites corresponding to PLP-binding sites in APO form – it can be therefore used to study the variability of PLP binding and detect conformational changes between apo and holo structures. The results also show solvent accessible surface area calculated for the binding site (SASA), and the SASA normalized by the number of amino acids in the binding site that allows the analysis of the binding sites. More detailed information and visualizations of apo holo pairs are available through the 'View' or 'Download' buttons.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Unique_protein_ligand_interactions_499">
    <rdfs:label>499 unique protein-ligand interactions</rdfs:label>
    <ahoj:reportedValue rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">499</ahoj:reportedValue>
    <ahoj:unit>unique protein-ligand interactions</ahoj:unit>
    <pred:represent rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Unique_ligands_193"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C094">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C094: PDBe_KB - provides information about - PDBe_KB_known_ligands_198</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/PDBe_KB_known_ligands_198"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/provides_information_about"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/PDBe_KB"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>PDBe-KB provides information about 198 known ligands for the same protein.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The search by UniProt AC provides an overview of known binding sites of the protein and a number of biologically relevant ligands found. It can be compared to the information provided by the PDBe-KB; if we look at glycogen phosphorylase from Oryctolagus cuniculus (UniProt AC: P00489 – see Figure 1) we can see 499 unique protein–ligand interactions – these represent 193 unique ligands, while PDBe-KB provides information about 198 known ligands – five of those are not biologically relevant according to BioLiP2 database. The result also shows us that the most common ligand is PLP that is forming 284 unique interactions in 130 structures. There are 16 sites corresponding to PLP-binding sites in APO form – it can be therefore used to study the variability of PLP binding and detect conformational changes between apo and holo structures. The results also show solvent accessible surface area calculated for the binding site (SASA), and the SASA normalized by the number of amino acids in the binding site that allows the analysis of the binding sites. More detailed information and visualizations of apo holo pairs are available through the 'View' or 'Download' buttons.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C069">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C069: AHoJ_DB - can support - Machine_learning_tool_development</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Machine_learning_tool_development"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/can_support"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>The database provides pocket-level apo/holo annotations that can be used to train or evaluate ligand-binding-site predictors. This is one of the motivating use cases behind the resource.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C027">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C027: AHoJ_DB_input_selection - requires maximum resolution - Resolution_threshold</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Resolution_threshold"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/requires_maximum_resolution"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB_input_selection"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Candidate structures were processed only if they satisfied a broad resolution cutoff of 7 Å. The authors used this permissive threshold to allow broad search coverage.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
