  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C105">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C105: Normalized_SASA - supports - Binding_site_analysis</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Binding_site_analysis"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/supports"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Normalized_SASA"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>SASA normalized by binding-site amino-acid count allows analysis of binding sites.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The search by UniProt AC provides an overview of known binding sites of the protein and a number of biologically relevant ligands found. It can be compared to the information provided by the PDBe-KB; if we look at glycogen phosphorylase from Oryctolagus cuniculus (UniProt AC: P00489 – see Figure 1) we can see 499 unique protein–ligand interactions – these represent 193 unique ligands, while PDBe-KB provides information about 198 known ligands – five of those are not biologically relevant according to BioLiP2 database. The result also shows us that the most common ligand is PLP that is forming 284 unique interactions in 130 structures. There are 16 sites corresponding to PLP-binding sites in APO form – it can be therefore used to study the variability of PLP binding and detect conformational changes between apo and holo structures. The results also show solvent accessible surface area calculated for the binding site (SASA), and the SASA normalized by the number of amino acids in the binding site that allows the analysis of the binding sites. More detailed information and visualizations of apo holo pairs are available through the 'View' or 'Download' buttons.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/can_be_ambiguous_for">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Property"/>
    <rdfs:label>can be ambiguous for</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C009">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C009: Pocket_centric_view - clarifies - Apo_holo_classification</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_holo_classification"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/clarifies"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Pocket_centric_view"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>The paper claims that a pocket-centric view is practical for clarifying whether a corresponding site is apo or holo. This is one of the conceptual foundations of AHoJ-DB.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C004">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C004: Machine_learning_tool_development - often use - Protein_structure</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Protein_structure"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/often_use"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Machine_learning_tool_development"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Training and testing of ligand-binding-site predictors is often performed on one holo structure as a representative. The paper presents this as unrealistic because the apo form may differ from the holo binding-site geometry.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C068">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C068: AHoJ_DB - can support - Drug_design_and_docking</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Drug_design_and_docking"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/can_support"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>AHoJ-DB can help drug-design and docking experiments by exposing apo/holo pocket variability. This may reduce reliance on a single rigid holo structure.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
