    <rdfs:label>lacks</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C024">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C024: Apo_pocket - lacks - Detected_ligand_near_pocket</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Detected_ligand_near_pocket"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/lacks"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_pocket"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>A mapped candidate pocket is classified as apo when no non-solvent ligand is detected near the mapped pocket. This does not mean the entire structure is ligand-free.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C047">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C047: Apo_pocket_in_filtered_subset - have higher - Pocket_geometry_metric</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Pocket_geometry_metric"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/have_higher"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_pocket_in_filtered_subset"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Subset 2 shows that apo pockets have consistently higher RMSD relative to the query pocket than holo pockets. The paper suggests this may point toward cryptic-site-like behavior.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/has_known_binding_site">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Property"/>
    <rdfs:label>has known binding site</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Multi_pocket_structure">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/Concept"/>
    <rdfs:label>Multi pocket structures</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:label>multi-pocket structure</rdfs:label>
    <skos:altLabel>Multi pocket structures</skos:altLabel>
    <ahoj:mergedEntityCount rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</ahoj:mergedEntityCount>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C080">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C080: PDB_search_use_case - can reveal - PDB_search_use_case</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/PDB_search_use_case"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/can_reveal"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/PDB_search_use_case"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>The paper says searching by PDB code can reveal information about the diversity of known bound ligands and binding modes. This is useful for inspecting structure-specific ligand binding.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C104">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C104: UniProt_AC_search - reports - Normalized_SASA</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Normalized_SASA"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/reports"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/UniProt_AC_search"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>The result shows SASA normalized by the number of amino acids in the binding site.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The search by UniProt AC provides an overview of known binding sites of the protein and a number of biologically relevant ligands found. It can be compared to the information provided by the PDBe-KB; if we look at glycogen phosphorylase from Oryctolagus cuniculus (UniProt AC: P00489 – see Figure 1) we can see 499 unique protein–ligand interactions – these represent 193 unique ligands, while PDBe-KB provides information about 198 known ligands – five of those are not biologically relevant according to BioLiP2 database. The result also shows us that the most common ligand is PLP that is forming 284 unique interactions in 130 structures. There are 16 sites corresponding to PLP-binding sites in APO form – it can be therefore used to study the variability of PLP binding and detect conformational changes between apo and holo structures. The results also show solvent accessible surface area calculated for the binding site (SASA), and the SASA normalized by the number of amino acids in the binding site that allows the analysis of the binding sites. More detailed information and visualizations of apo holo pairs are available through the 'View' or 'Download' buttons.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C044">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C044: Apo_pocket_in_filtered_subset - have more - Mapped_binding_residue_metric</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Mapped_binding_residue_metric"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/have_more"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_pocket_in_filtered_subset"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>In Subset 1, apo pockets showed a much higher percentage of unobserved mapped binding residues than holo pockets. The paper interprets this as evidence that apo pockets are less likely to have ordered structural representation.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_holo_pair_visualizations">
    <rdfs:label>apo-holo pair visualizations</rdfs:label>
    <pred:are_available_through rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Download_button"/>
    <pred:are_available_through rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/View_button"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C021">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C021: Mapped_candidate_pocket - may differ geometrically from - Query_pocket</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Query_pocket"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/may_differ_geometrically_from"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Mapped_candidate_pocket"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Although mapped candidate pockets correspond to the same sequence region, their local geometry can differ. AHoJ-DB records metrics such as RMSD, volume, SASA, and b-factor to capture these differences.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C002">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C002: Apo_structure_and_holo_structure - are essential for - Protein_conformational_variability</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Protein_conformational_variability"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/are_essential_for"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_structure_and_holo_structure"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Apo and holo forms provide complementary views of the same protein. Comparing them helps expose structural changes associated with ligand binding.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/C036">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>C036: Structural_factor - occur within - Mapped_candidate_pocket</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Mapped_candidate_pocket"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/occur_within"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Structural_factor"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Orthosteric factors are ligands, solvent molecules, or PTMs found within the mapped pocket residues. They directly affect interpretation of the local binding site.</ahoj:claimText>
  </rdf:Description>
